  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 249 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nThe pattern turns quite active starting Sunday night, as deep layer \nmoisture increases ahead of an approaching 500 mb trough and \nmultiple subsequent waves keep the gulf moisture stream open through \nthe first part of the week. The first day of concern will be Monday \nas the aforementioned 500 mb trough passes through the northern\nCONUS. This will bring a push of low level moisture and \nincreasing temperatures up into Iowa, along with strong mid and \nupper level flow. Instability values in our area will maximized \nMonday afternoon, with plenty of shear for organized storms. \nFortunately, a warm nose aloft will work to keep the atmosphere \ncapped off and activity surface based, at least at this lead \ntime. Still, strong 850 mb winds could certainly support some \ndamaging winds. The storm prediction center has already started \nto key in on Monday, with a Day 4 15% risk over central Iowa. \n\nThe active weather continues through at least Tuesday and Wednesday, \nalthough the pattern looks to transition to more of heavy rainfall \nthreat through these next two days. Modeled PWATs around 1.9 to 2\" \nwill be conducive for efficient rainfall, and multiple days of \nconvection along a quasi-stationary boundary could lead to some \nflooding concerns. It's still a bit early to get too ingrained \nin details, as mesoscale factors could cause this boundary to \nsink or move around, but the current synoptic setup certainly \npoints towards potential hydrology concerns through next week. \nThe weather prediction center is already on top of this as well,\nwith slight risks for excessive rainfall spanning from Sunday \nto Tuesday of next week.",
